// @run
// @log 1
// @log 7
// THE TWO STRUCT-ROW RESOLVERS NOW AGREE ABOUT AN ADOPTED LITERAL (D461).
//
// `structIndexOfObjCtx` matches `{ r: 7 }` onto the declared `Circle` row through
// `anonValueFitsField`'s union-box leniency ("a union BOX field accepts every atom by
// construction"), and that adoption SUPPRESSES the `#anon` row `collectAnonShapes` would have
// minted — its gate is `structIndexOfObj(ai) < 0`. So the shape the checker named, `{r: i32}`,
// has exactly one row in the table and it is Circle's.
//
// `structIndexOfTypeName` then refused that same pairing as a proven code mismatch (`want` 0
// for the spelled `i32`, `code` 16 for the box), so the render named NO row, `mvValKindOfName`
// answered its `-3` unsupported sentinel, `mvShapeOfMapNodeTy` minted no mv slot, and
// `letMapShapeOf` handed `emitMapNew` a `-3`: `emitProgram: unsupported map value type`, on a
// program whose value rep the emitter would have got right. Deleting the unused `type Circle`
// line made it run — that is how load-bearing the disagreement was.
//
// D207 (#2006) closed the half of this floor that HAS a destination, by letting
// `letMapDestShape` be consulted at `-3`. That rung is a FIND and interns nothing, so it
// cannot help here: there is no destination, so there is no slot to find. The fix is one
// container up, at the resolver that decides which row the value's shape names.
//
// The SECOND read is the point of the second `@log`: the value comes back out through
// `memReadUnboxAtomKind` (D209's close), so the map does not merely construct — the adopted
// box unboxes to the atom the checker typed the read as.
type Circle = { r: i32 | null }
const lv1 = Map()
lv1["k0"] = { r: 7 }
print(lv1.size)
print((lv1["k0"] ?? { r: 0 }).r)
